WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.473
This is the awful truth.

00:00:09.452 --> 00:00:11.790
There's always someone in
the family that's pushing it.

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:23.580
This lady does not deserve
to be taken advantage of.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:28.200
Especially at your age,
with your own grandchild.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:29.260
Yeah.

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:31.570
My grandma is having to sue them.

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:33.590
I'm testifying against them.

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:34.700
Nobody's talking.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:36.450
They're one floor below right now.

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:38.840
I get afraid to go on the elevator.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:42.460
It's sad that it had to devolve to this.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:43.775
It's like a soap opera to me.

00:00:43.775 --> 00:00:45.520
It's a very sad thing.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:47.560
It's a family feud.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:49.320
A family feud.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:52.190
Here's a family that on
paper you would think

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:54.570
has the wealth that so many people seek,

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:56.170
yet at the end of the day,

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:58.503
it seemed to sort of rip everybody apart.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:09.160
My name is Tom Schoenberg.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:12.030
I'm a senior reporter
here at Bloomberg News.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:13.620
Cover financial enforcement.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:15.770
I was approached by someone close

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:17.600
to the Schottenstein family

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:21.550
and asked whether or not I
would be interested in writing

00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:25.670
about Beverly Schottenstein
and this case that she had

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:27.380
against JP Morgan.

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:29.000
And it was immediately clear

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:31.703
that there was a great
story here to be told.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:36.510
So I started just assembling
all sorts of documents

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:38.380
that the family made available to me.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:42.230
That included a diary that
Beverly had begun writing

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:44.210
when she started having suspicions

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:46.673
about what was actually
going on with her money.

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:49.400
Beverly obtained her wealth

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:51.630
from the Schottenstein retail empire

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:55.710
that has at times included
Big Lots, Value City,

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:59.010
American Eagle Outfitters, DSW Shoes.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.340
Beverly's husband died, and at which point

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:03.630
they sold off their
portion of the company,

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:07.180
bringing them an enormous
amount of wealth at around 1990.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:10.500
Estimates of her wealth
were around $90 million.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.520
She for many years had that pool of money

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:15.890
being managed by outside advisors.

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:19.980
Her grandsons in the late
2000s had both graduated

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.880
and were becoming financial advisors.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:24.600
She gave them a little bit to work with,

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:29.130
and over time her grandson,
Evan, became the trustee

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:30.743
of entire estate.

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:36.390
Managing family money is not a conflict.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:38.340
In fact, some ways in wealth management,

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:39.630
it's kind of how you get your start.

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:42.840
It's kind of the seed that
you need to sort of get going

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:43.710
in the business.

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:46.230
But Beverly started having suspicions

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:49.763
that something was wrong
probably in around 2016.

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:55.960
Well, there were suspicious
things, but taken separately

00:02:56.180 --> 00:03:00.340
without everyone talking
about their own experiences,

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:02.810
it was hard to really put it together.

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:07.290
She had written a check
to her caretaker, Dawn,

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:08.390
that bounced.

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:11.650
So she went down to
their local Chase branch

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:13.510
to sort of find out what was going on.

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:16.320
She wasn't getting paper
statements in the mail.

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:19.540
So she asked them to print
out a number of her statements

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:21.257
having to do with her checking account

00:03:21.257 --> 00:03:22.940
and her credit card account.

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:26.980
So we went to the bank and
asked for the statements

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:28.890
and she got a whole year

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:33.093
and going through all of
them page by page by page.

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:35.510
And it was there she
saw all sorts of charges

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:39.100
that she said she didn't
authorize or didn't know about.

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:42.600
I was becoming very, very suspicious.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:45.340
I was starting to look in the money part

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:47.144
and that's what bothered me more

00:03:47.144 --> 00:03:52.144
when I saw that money was going
and I was not a big spender.

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:55.280
Around the same time, Dawn,

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:57.500
she starts noticing Beverly's son,

00:03:57.500 --> 00:03:59.190
who live in the condominium below her,

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:02.070
came up with Evan with a shredder

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:05.650
and started grabbing
papers in her office area,

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:08.550
including some with JP Morgan letterhead,

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:09.970
and just started sitting
at the kitchen table

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:12.700
just shredding documents over and over.

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:14.890
Yeah, they came in one morning.

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:16.240
They used to do it the back.

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:18.330
Through the back door,

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:21.120
and they brought this shredder inside.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:24.160
And then they went into
the drawers in the office

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:28.800
and took out all the papers,
and they start to doing it.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:32.160
I was very suspicious because
I know you don't go around

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:34.830
and getting destroying papers.

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:36.550
Beverly said that they had done this

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:38.340
many times before.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:41.157
That's when she said to me,

00:04:41.157 --> 00:04:41.990
"You know, Dawn,

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:45.420
"I'm going to write my
feelings in this black book."

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:46.253
A diary.

00:04:46.253 --> 00:04:49.170
It's a diary and she
started writing each day.

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:51.432
She would write a little at a time.

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:57.000
Around the holidays of
2018, a package had arrived,

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:00.630
a FedEx package, and in it were materials

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:02.630
about a venture capital fund

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:04.160
based out of the Cayman Islands.

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:07.097
Dawn and some other family
members take a look at this

00:05:07.097 --> 00:05:11.260
and they see that Beverly
is signed up for this fund

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:16.020
to the tune of $5 million over
the course of several years.

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:18.430
And that was really the beginning of it.

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:21.650
That was sort of the tip of the iceberg

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:25.050
when it became obvious that
something was going on,

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:27.723
that my grandmother really
wasn't aware of this fund.

00:05:29.170 --> 00:05:30.440
Beverly started to panic.

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:31.720
She just got really upset

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:34.690
and called JP Morgan's
headquarters in New York

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:36.670
and asked for Jamie Diamond.

00:05:36.670 --> 00:05:39.523
She said she was told
someone would call her back,

00:05:39.523 --> 00:05:42.290
but she says that that never happened.

00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:45.160
She wrote a letter to Evan and Avi,

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:48.160
and in there, she laid
out a number of things

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:49.730
that were concerning her,

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:53.260
unauthorized trading,
the checking account.

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:56.640
She had bought a million
dollars worth of jewelry

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:58.750
in a safe deposit box.

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:02.040
And that her son Bobby
had the key to that box.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:04.220
And that that jewelry had gone missing.

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:05.680
All this was kind of packaged

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:10.680
in this five-page handwritten
amendment to a will and trust.

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:12.580
There's such an evolving narrative

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:14.460
about why the jewelry was taken.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:17.880
The only truth we really know
is that the jewelry is gone,

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:19.600
but why it happened,

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:21.900
there've been a lot of different stories.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:26.660
You just don't take your
mother's wonderful things

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:28.540
and hand it out.

00:06:28.540 --> 00:06:31.900
And they emailed that note to JP Morgan

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:34.500
to cease all activity on her account.

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:38.240
And it ended up setting off
a fire storm after that.

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:41.040
As soon as they got this email

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:42.620
that there was a cease and desist

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:44.835
and that she wanted to send her money

00:06:44.835 --> 00:06:47.240
and change brokerage firms,

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:50.700
they were actually vacationing
one floor below downstairs.

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:52.040
They're there right now.

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:55.310
The next day, Beverly's in her place.

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:57.760
The granddaughter's still
in her place visiting.

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:01.390
Dawn is there, and up comes Bobby

00:07:01.390 --> 00:07:03.360
kind of barging through the door.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:06.600
And he's screaming about what
was sent to the JP Morgan.

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:08.860
They're now investigating them.

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:11.510
And he's furious, takes his mother,

00:07:11.510 --> 00:07:15.650
and sort of puts her into a
chair, gets pen and paper.

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:18.930
And she says physically forced her

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:21.290
to write a retraction letter.

00:07:21.290 --> 00:07:23.077
They were pushing me around.

00:07:23.077 --> 00:07:24.500
It was a scary time.

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:26.270
I was really afraid too.

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:28.460
I had to take her to the doctor,

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:32.950
and she did an x-ray because
she was in so much pain.

00:07:32.950 --> 00:07:34.640
The locks had to be changed.

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:36.370
The phone number had to be changed.

00:07:36.370 --> 00:07:39.170
I really thought we were
going to have to hire guards

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:42.030
to be outside just for protection.

00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:43.360
Oh, it was terrible.

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:44.293
Horrible scene.

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:51.300
Beverly brought her case
before the financial industry

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:54.420
regulatory authorities,
usually known as FINRA.

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:56.600
It took several months of hearings.

00:07:56.600 --> 00:08:00.130
She sees all sorts of
transactions that's going on.

00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:03.640
Hundreds of them that she claims
that she never knew about,

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:07.550
never authorized, but yet were
logged to JP Morgan's files

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:10.090
as having been direct requests from her.

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:13.760
That included the sale of a
large amount of Apple stock

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:15.370
worth several million dollars,

00:08:15.370 --> 00:08:17.980
as well as all her stock in Big Lots.

00:08:17.980 --> 00:08:21.410
Over the course of their
time with JP Morgan,

00:08:21.410 --> 00:08:23.740
Evan and Avi were making
all sorts of trading

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:27.460
on her account that was
generating millions of dollars

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:29.740
in commissions for themselves in the bank,

00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:33.650
yet she was not sort of getting
any of the type of gains

00:08:33.650 --> 00:08:36.910
that you would expect
an $80 million account

00:08:36.910 --> 00:08:39.230
in a bullish market to be able to get.

00:08:39.230 --> 00:08:41.320
They issued a ruling entirely in her favor

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:44.820
finding that her grandsons
as well as JP Morgan,

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:47.650
they were liable for violating
their fiduciary duty,

00:08:47.650 --> 00:08:49.510
misrepresenting themselves.

00:08:49.510 --> 00:08:53.190
And they also found that
the bank and her grandson,

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:55.780
Evan, were liable for elder abuse.

00:08:55.780 --> 00:08:58.490
Evan and Avi, they were
fired from JP Morgan.

00:08:58.490 --> 00:09:02.500
They issued an award of about $19 million.

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:05.420
I reached out for comment,
and JP Morgan emphasized

00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:08.540
that Evan and Avi were
no longer with the firm

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:10.940
and that their actions do not represent

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:13.030
the values of the bank.

00:09:13.030 --> 00:09:16.060
I reached out to Even and Avi,
heard back from their lawyer

00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:20.910
and saying that they disagree
with the law and the facts

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:23.730
that FINRA used to decide
the case against them

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:26.063
and are seeing if they
can have it withdrawn.

00:09:27.380 --> 00:09:29.790
Her son, Bobby, wrote her a letter.

00:09:29.790 --> 00:09:31.853
He said his sons weren't criminals.

00:09:32.727 --> 00:09:35.030
And he also admits to taking her jewelry,

00:09:35.030 --> 00:09:36.850
and he says he needed it to pay off

00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:39.640
some bad business debts he had.

00:09:39.640 --> 00:09:42.810
They never once came up to say I'm sorry.

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:47.780
If there had been an
apology and some acceptance

00:09:47.780 --> 00:09:51.680
and asking for forgiveness
from my grandmother,

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:54.660
I don't think we'd be in the
position that we are now.

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:55.760
Since the piece came out,

00:09:55.760 --> 00:10:00.180
we've received dozens
of messages from people,

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:03.882
many of whom are telling
their own personal tales

00:10:03.882 --> 00:10:07.480
of financial advisors gone bad

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:12.480
or family members who
got control of an estate

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:15.860
and abused those within.

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:16.693
Seems sad.

00:10:16.693 --> 00:10:19.180
It's really resonated
with a lot of people.

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:23.010
I keep hearing a lot of
feedback about Beverly

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:26.740
and how a lot of people feel
she did the right thing.

00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:29.070
You're never too old to
stand up for yourself

00:10:29.070 --> 00:10:31.230
and try to right an injustice.

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:34.280
She was adamant that she
wanted to stand up for herself,

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:37.733
and at the end of the day, they did wrong.

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:40.580
It's a painful life lesson.

00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:44.783
Beverly taught them that
you don't mess with grandma.

